@inject ApiMetadata Api

@* AppSidebar's Overview, written by hand after AppShell's: the drawer on phones, what's pinned at
   the top and bottom, a panel under the header, the icon rail (and from C#) and a full-height
   sidebar, each a page of its own in a frame with a phone width. Parts, Accessibility and See also
   come from the metadata and the doc comments; See also adds the demos. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A sidebar is the column of links beside the page in an <Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor>. On phones it becomes a drawer that the <Code>SidebarToggle</Code> in the header opens. Both work <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">A drawer on phones</Heading>
<TextBlock>On narrow screens the sidebar becomes a drawer, and a menu button in the header opens it. To set the width below which this happens, set <Code>Breakpoint</Code>. It defaults to 768px. The menu button shows only below it.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="app-sidebar-drawer" WidthSwitch />
<TextBlock>To open the drawer from the right, with the menu button at the right of the header as content sites do, set <Code>DrawerPosition="SidebarPosition.End"</Code>. To move the whole sidebar to the right, set <Code>Position</Code>. <Code>Width</Code> sets the sidebar's width; it defaults to 16rem.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@FromTheRight</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Pinned at the top and bottom</Heading>
<TextBlock>A sidebar can pin content at its top and bottom while the links between them scroll. Put what stays at the top, such as a workspace, in a <Code>SidebarHeader</Code>. Put what stays at the bottom, such as the account, in a <Code>SidebarFooter</Code>. Write both directly in the sidebar.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Pinned</Code>
<TextBlock>In the drawer, the header shares the top row with the close button. With no <Code>SidebarHeader</Code>, the drawer's top row shows the shell's <Code>AppBrand</Code>. Links written straight in the sidebar are its body, so use <Code>SidebarBody</Code> only for spacing, such as a <Code>Gap</Code> between two lists.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">A panel under the header</Heading>
<TextBlock>On phones, the sidebar can open as a full-width panel under the header instead of a drawer. The header stays in view, and the menu button becomes the panel's close button. Set <Code>SmallScreen="SidebarSmallScreen.Panel"</Code> to turn it on.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="app-sidebar-panel" WidthSwitch />
<TextBlock>A <Anchor Href="docs/components/site-nav">SiteNav</Anchor> in the header puts its links at the top of the panel or drawer on phones, so a phone has one menu rather than two.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">An icon rail</Heading>
<TextBlock>An icon rail is the sidebar shrunk to its icons on wide screens, which leaves more room for the page. The menu button collapses and expands it, and each link's text shows as a tooltip. Set <Code>Collapsible</Code> to turn it on.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="app-sidebar-icon-rail" WidthSwitch />
<List>
    <ListItem>With <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">IzzyBlazor's script</Anchor>, the user's choice is kept in a cookie that the server reads, so the next page arrives collapsed. The cookie is named after the sidebar's <Code>Id</Code>: <Code>rail-collapsed</Code> in this example, <Code>ib-sidebar-collapsed</Code> by default. To turn this off, set <Code>RememberState="false"</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>Collapsed</Code> sets how the sidebar starts when no choice is remembered.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>In a shell with no menu button, a chevron in the sidebar's footer row collapses it. To show or hide that chevron whatever the header has, set <Code>CollapseButton</Code>.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Binding the collapsed state</Heading>
<TextBlock>On interactive pages, you can read and set whether the rail is collapsed from C#, as a focus-mode button does. Set <Code>@@bind-Collapsed</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="app-sidebar-bound-collapse" WidthSwitch />

<Heading Level="2">Full height</Heading>
<TextBlock>A full-height sidebar runs the full height of the window, with the header and footer beside it rather than above and below. Set <Code>FullHeight</Code> to turn it on. For a dark sidebar in a light app, also set <Code>ColorScheme="ColorScheme.Dark"</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="app-sidebar-full-height" WidthSwitch />
<TextBlock>A brand in the <Code>SidebarHeader</Code> lines up with the header beside it.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>The sidebar and its toggle have names for screen readers. The defaults are below.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Names</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>Label</Code></td><td>The navigation landmark</td><td>"Main"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>CloseLabel</Code></td><td>The drawer's close button</td><td>"Close menu"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>CollapseLabel</Code></td><td>The collapse control</td><td>"Collapse sidebar"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>
<TextBlock><Code>SidebarToggle</Code> has its own <Code>Label</Code> ("Open menu"), <Code>CloseLabel</Code> and <Code>CollapseLabel</Code>.</TextBlock>

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <AppShell>
            <AppHeader>
                <SidebarToggle />
                <AppBrand Href="/" Icon="Lucide.Zap" Label="Acme" />
            </AppHeader>
            <AppSidebar>
                <NavMenu>
                    <NavItem Href="/" Icon="Lucide.House">Home</NavItem>
                    <NavItem Href="/projects" Icon="Lucide.Folder">Projects</NavItem>
                </NavMenu>
            </AppSidebar>
            <AppMain>@Body</AppMain>
        </AppShell>
        """;

    private const string FromTheRight = """
        @* On the left on wide screens; on phones the drawer opens from the right,
           and the menu button sits at the right of the header. *@
        <AppSidebar DrawerPosition="SidebarPosition.End" Breakpoint="Breakpoint.Lg">
            <NavMenu>...</NavMenu>
        </AppSidebar>
        """;

    private const string Pinned = """
        <AppSidebar>
            <SidebarHeader>
                <AppBrand Href="/" Icon="Lucide.Zap" Label="Acme" />
            </SidebarHeader>
            <SidebarBody Gap="4">
                <NavMenu>...</NavMenu>
                <NavMenu aria-label="Projects">...</NavMenu>
            </SidebarBody>
            <SidebarFooter>
                <NavMenu aria-label="Account">
                    <NavItem Href="/account" Icon="Lucide.CircleUser">Ada Lovelace</NavItem>
                </NavMenu>
            </SidebarFooter>
        </AppSidebar>
        """;
}
